First — quick clarification: you wrote your last blood pressure was "1.2." Could you confirm what that means (for example, 120/82 mmHg or something else)? That will change how aggressive to be.

Below I’ve put together: (A) what to think about for hypertension targets and treatment given your conditions, (B) when a cardiology referral is reasonable (and what to ask if you want one sent to Colorado Cardiology), and (C) practical items/tests to request at your PCP visit and a short script you can use with Dr. Chen. Tell me if you want me to draft an email or message to Dr. Chen or prepare a referral request to Colorado Cardiology.

A. Hypertension — key considerations for you
- BP target: For most older adults (including age 60) current major guidelines typically aim for a systolic goal around <130 mmHg if tolerated and if not frail. Given you have atrial fibrillation and retinopathy (evidence of end-organ vascular disease), tighter control (e.g., <130/80) is reasonable if it can be achieved safely without orthostatic symptoms.
- Confirm the reading: ask whether “1.2” was a typo — make sure you and Dr. Chen are working from accurate, properly measured readings (see measurement checklist below).
- Orthostatic hypotension: because you take metoprolol, check standing/lying BP and heart rate. Older adults and those on multiple cardiovascular meds can get symptomatic drops.
- Resistant or uncontrolled HTN: If BP is >130/80 despite lifestyle changes and two meds at optimal doses (one usually a diuretic or ACEi/ARB), that suggests need for intensification and possibly specialty input.
- Target organ damage: history of retinopathy already indicates microvascular damage. That strengthens the case for tighter control and for assessing kidneys (urine albumin) and other organs.
- Sleep apnea: untreated OSA raises BP and makes hypertension harder to control and increases AF burden — optimizing CPAP/adherence can improve BP and AF outcomes.

B. When to consider referral to cardiology (or Colorado Cardiology)
You do NOT automatically need a cardiology referral based on age alone. Consider cardiology if any of these apply:
- Recurrent or poorly controlled AF symptoms despite current therapy (palpitations, lightheadedness, syncope), or difficulty controlling rate on metoprolol.
- Consideration of rhythm-control strategies (cardioversion or ablation) or complex decisions about anticoagulation beyond standard guidance.
- Uncontrolled hypertension despite 2–3 antihypertensives at maximally tolerated doses (i.e., suspected resistant hypertension).
- Evidence of structural heart disease (heart failure symptoms, reduced ejection fraction on prior echo, murmurs) or abnormal ECG.
- Frequent or unexplained presyncope/syncope, or concern for bradycardia from metoprolol.
- You want a second opinion about risk and aggressive BP targets given your retinopathy and AF.

If none of the above apply and your BP is controlled, continuing with Dr. Chen for HTN management is reasonable. If you prefer proactive cardiology review (given AF and age), a single consultation for echocardiogram and rhythm assessment is reasonable.

C. Tests and information to request at your PCP visit
- Confirm accurate office BP (seated, 5 minutes rest) and orthostatic BP (lying → standing after 1 and 3 minutes).
- Ask for ambulatory 24-hour BP monitoring (ABPM) or home BP logs if there is any discrepancy or suspected white-coat/masked HTN.
- ECG (if not recent) and consider echocardiogram if no echo in past few years or if new symptoms.
- Labs: basic metabolic panel (BMP) including creatinine and potassium (renal function matters for apixaban dosing and ACEi/ARB use), urine albumin/creatinine ratio (for kidney damage), fasting lipid panel, HbA1c if indicated.
- Review apixaban dosing and renal function: apixaban dose can be affected by kidney function and weight/age criteria — ensure 5 mg BID is still appropriate.
- Check CPAP adherence and any sleep study info — ask if improved sleep apnea control is being addressed as part of BP/AF management.
- Ophthalmology follow-up for retinopathy — ensure coordination because anticoagulation can affect ocular bleeding risk and BP control affects retinopathy progression.

D. Medication considerations and adjustments
- First-line combinations often include an ACE inhibitor or ARB + a thiazide-type diuretic and/or calcium channel blocker. Beta-blocker (metoprolol) is useful for rate control in AF but is not typically first choice for isolated HTN control in older adults unless other indications (like CAD, LV dysfunction, AF).
- If BP is not at goal, discuss adding an ACEi/ARB or thiazide-like diuretic or amlodipine, considering kidney function and potassium.
- Monitor for interactions/side effects: apixaban mostly has renal clearance considerations — check creatinine. Metoprolol may increase fatigue, affect exercise tolerance, and could worsen some aspects of sleep apnea or daytime sleepiness in some people (discuss symptoms).
- If you are on only metoprolol for BP control, you may need a different/added antihypertensive to reach targets intentionally.

E. Practical items to bring/prepare and monitoring
- Bring a 1–2 week home BP log (morning and evening readings), list of current meds (including supplements), CPAP usage report (if you use CPAP).
- Note symptoms: palpitations, dizziness/lightheadedness, shortness of breath, chest pain, bleeding, vision changes.
- If you’d like, ask for ABPM to confirm average daytime/nighttime BP and assess nocturnal dipping.
- Frequency of monitoring: if meds change or renal function at risk, check BMP within 1–2 weeks of initiation/change and again at 3 months.

F. Suggested questions to ask Dr. Sarah Chen
- “Can we clarify my last BP reading? Is it 120/82 (or was that a typo)? What should my target BP be given my AF and retinopathy?”
- “Are my home BP readings aligned with office readings? Should we do 24-hour ambulatory BP monitoring?”
