Below is a structured prep you can use for your visit with Dr. Sarah Chen (Central Austin Primary Care): why things matter, what to check, questions to ask about hypertension management, and when a referral to Colorado Cardiology would be appropriate.

Why these issues matter together (brief)
- Atrial fibrillation (on apixaban) — raises stroke risk; good BP control reduces stroke and bleeding risk on anticoagulants.
- Sleep apnea — commonly raises blood pressure and increases AF recurrence; treating sleep apnea (CPAP) often helps both BP and AF control.
- Retinopathy — indicates microvascular damage and increases the importance of tight BP control to prevent worsening eye disease.
- Metoprolol — helps with AF rate control but is not always the best sole agent for BP control; you may need additional/alternate antihypertensives.

BP targets (talk with Dr. Chen to individualize)
- Current guideline-based approach for people with cardiovascular disease or high ASCVD risk is typically a systolic target around <130 mm Hg if tolerated. Targets may be individualized based on age, frailty, orthostatic symptoms, and comorbidities.
- For patients on anticoagulation, uncontrolled very high BP (e.g., >160 systolic) increases intracranial bleeding risk; keeping BP controlled reduces that risk.
- Ask Dr. Chen whether <130/80 or a slightly higher target is best for you given your retinopathy and AF.

Tests and data to bring/request
- Home BP log (several readings morning and evening for 1–2 weeks) or consider ambulatory BP monitoring.
- Recent weight, symptoms (dizziness, syncope, palpitations, worsening shortness of breath), and activity tolerance.
- Labs: basic metabolic panel (creatinine, electrolytes), CBC (if concerned about bleeding/ anemia), lipid profile, A1c if diabetic or at risk, urine albumin/creatinine ratio (kidney damage).
