You do not automatically need cardiology because of age alone, but consider referral if any of these apply:
- AF that is symptomatic despite rate control (palpitations, syncope, presyncope, limiting symptoms).
- Poor rate control on current meds (resting HR persistently high or low despite adjustments).
- Consideration of rhythm-control strategies (antiarrhythmic meds, cardioversion, catheter ablation).
- Recurrent thromboembolism or complex anticoagulation decisions despite guideline therapy.
- Structural heart disease, heart failure, reduced LVEF, or abnormal echocardiogram.
- Resistant or difficult-to-control hypertension (BP above target on ≥3 medications of complementary classes, including a diuretic).
- Concerning arrhythmias on monitoring or syncope of cardiac origin.
If none of the above apply, PCP management with possible input from nephrology/ophthalmology (for retinopathy) and sleep medicine (for sleep apnea) is reasonable.

D — Tests and monitoring to request now
- Home BP monitoring: log morning and evening BPs for 1–2 weeks (validated home device).
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, electrolytes) before or after starting/adjusting ACE/ARB or diuretics.
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney damage marker).
- Lipid panel and A1c (cardiovascular risk management).
- ECG (if none within last year) and consider ambulatory rhythm monitor if you’ve had palpitations/syncope.
- Echocardiogram if not done recently or if there are signs of structural heart disease or heart failure.
- Retinal exam with ophthalmology (given history of retinopathy) — let them know BP and anticoagulation status.
- CPAP adherence data from sleep medicine (to correlate with BP control).

